[HEADING=2]What made the difference[/HEADING]

[LIST]
[*][B]16k TLS records.[/B] [ICODE]ssl_buffer_size 1400[/ICODE] fits one MTU but turns a 15.6 KB cached page into ~11 TLS records (and ~11 write syscalls). [ICODE]16k[/ICODE] sends it as one: [B]151,438 → 442,765 req/s[/B] on the same page and TLS stack. This is the whole Stack 3 gap and more.
[*][B]8 Angie workers on 12 CPUs.[/B] [ICODE]worker_processes auto[/ICODE] (12) spent cores on TLS contention: 348–352k cached req/s. Two workers per three CPUs (8) gives [B]389–390k[/B], with member/thread tiers unchanged (764–767 / 840–843 req/s).
[*][B]Anubis with the Go collector out of the way.[/B] [ICODE]GOGC=off[/ICODE] with [ICODE]GOMEMLIMIT=2GiB[/ICODE] (the limit still bounds the heap; RSS < 2 GiB): 51,949 → [B]57,082[/B] req/s through the filter. The static-asset shadow keeps CSS/JS/images off the proxy on top of that.
[*][B]OPcache huge pages.[/B] [ICODE]vm.nr_hugepages[/ICODE] sized from RAM plus [ICODE]opcache.huge_code_pages=1[/ICODE]: +3.5 % on member/thread (808 → 835, 645 → 670 req/s).
[*][B]The rest of the Ubuntu profile[/B] (already present): tracing JIT, [ICODE]pm=static[/ICODE] one worker per CPU, gzip stored in the FastCGI cache, [ICODE]aio off[/ICODE] + [ICODE]brotli off[/ICODE] on cache hits, Unix sockets end to end, MariaDB pool-of-threads, XF data cache in Valkey.
[*][B]Sweeps ruled out the usual suspects:[/B] 24/32 PHP workers were slower than 12 (536/534 vs 548 req/s), JIT 128 MB and altered hotness thresholds were noise, [ICODE]validate_timestamps=0[/ICODE] was noise, Anubis [ICODE]GOMAXPROCS[/ICODE] 12/16 was within noise of 8.
[/LIST]

[HEADING=2]Why CentMinMod was competitive[/HEADING]

[LIST]
[*]PHP is compiled with [ICODE]-O3 -march=native[/ICODE] (CentMinMod) vs generic Sury packages: roughly 5–10 % per-request advantage.
[*]30 [ICODE]ondemand[/ICODE] PHP-FPM children over TCP hide database waits better than one static worker per CPU — that is why the 18-post thread page is still slightly behind, while member-home is 25–38 % ahead on Ubuntu.
[*]Nginx 1.31.8 with newer OpenSSL and [ICODE]sendfile_max_chunk 2m[/ICODE], [ICODE]tcp_nodelay[/ICODE], [ICODE]open_file_cache[/ICODE].
[*]Stock CentMinMod has no FastCGI page cache and no Anubis; those were added for the benchmark. Its 6 Nginx workers and JIT-disabled PHP leave performance unused.
[/LIST]

[HEADING=2]Caveats[/HEADING]

[LIST]
[*]CentMinMod’s page-cache config was a minimal addition, not its stock vhost; Ubuntu was tuned end to end. This measures the platforms, not identical configs.
[*]Thread-page throughput is page-size sensitive. The CentMinMod dataset averages 10.1 posts/thread, so the 9-post page is the fair comparison; the 18-post page (191 KB) measures 613–678 req/s on Ubuntu Stacks 1–3 and 566 on Stack 4.
[*]Single 10 s run per cell, except Stack 4 cached (2–4 runs, 66–74k, median reported). Cache-hit variance on this host is ±5 %.
[*]Stack 4 cached uses [ICODE]ab[/ICODE] and dynamic tiers use [ICODE]oha[/ICODE] because wrk 4.1.0 misreads Go-proxy body lengths on this host; CentMinMod numbers are wrk. Cached tiers are server-bound and the tools agree within 1–2 % on Stacks 1–3.
[*]PHP 8.6 RC5 is not in the matrix (CentMinMod EL10 supports up to 8.5); an earlier audit measured ~+6 % thread throughput on 8.6 RC5, offset by missing redis/imagick packages.
[/LIST]

[HEADING=2]Configuration highlights[/HEADING]

[CODE]
# h5bp/tls/ssl_engine.conf  (the 3x cached-tier change)
ssl_buffer_size 16k;

# angie.conf  (2/3 of CPUs; 8 on a 12-thread host)
worker_processes 8;
worker_cpu_affinity auto;
worker_rlimit_nofile 131072;
worker_connections 32768;

# Anubis unit (>=16 GB hosts; GOMEMLIMIT still bounds the heap)
Environment=GOGC=off
Environment=GOMEMLIMIT=2048MiB
Environment=GOMAXPROCS=8

# kernel + OPcache
vm.nr_hugepages = 479
opcache.huge_code_pages = 1
opcache.jit = tracing
opcache.jit_buffer_size = 64M

# PHP-FPM (12 threads)
pm = static
pm.max_children = 12
listen = /run/php/php-fpm.sock
[/CODE]

[HEADING=2]Conclusion[/HEADING]

On the same EPYC 4245P the Ubuntu 26.04 + Angie stack is now ahead on every measured tier: [B]+147 %[/B] cached guest throughput (390,232 vs 157,957 req/s), [B]+14.6 %[/B] with Anubis (68,725 vs 59,958), [B]+38 %[/B] member home (747 vs 542) and [B]+21 %[/B] member thread (838 vs 694). The decisive change was not a new service — it was one TLS directive, one worker-count rule, a Go runtime profile and PHP huge pages, all config-only and reproducible with [ICODE]setup.sh[/ICODE] + [ICODE]--optimize[/ICODE].

[CENTER][SIZE=1]Ubuntu 26.04.1 · Angie 1.12.4 · PHP 8.2–8.5 · MariaDB 11.8.6 · Valkey 9.0.4 · Anubis 1.28.2 · XenForo 2.3.13[/SIZE][/CENTER]
